# The two asset trees are *content*, not source: git must never rewrite their
# line endings.
#
# `assets/en/**` has a byte-identity contract with the upstream checkout, and two
# gates hash it (`english-byte-identity`, and `translation-freshness` through
# `sourceSha256`). Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in the system
# gitconfig, which converts LF to CRLF on checkout. `git status` does not show
# it, because git normalises CRLF back to LF when comparing — so the tree looks
# clean while `pnpm check` fails. Measured on a fresh clone before this file
# existed: 279 of 279 English assets came out CRLF and 264 no longer matched
# upstream.
#
# `-text` keeps the working tree byte-for-byte equal to what upstream ships,
# which is NOT the same as `eol=lf`: some upstream files are CRLF, and forcing LF
# on them would break the very identity this is protecting.
assets/en/** -text
assets/zh/** -text
